# Hosting Cost Profile vs. Lincoln Colocation / Dedicated — 2026-11-15

Task 28.6.9/8.6.11/6.5.9. Question (Will, 2026-11-15): would co-located or dedicated
hosting in Lincoln, NE on a fiber site be cheaper than the current setup for hosting
the web properties?

**Short answer: No.** The cheapest published Lincoln colocation price is $150/mo —
1.5× the ~$100/mo AWS anchor — before hardware. The cheapest published dedicated
rental in a Nebraska facility is ~$499/mo. The cloud VM wins on price, and it isn't
close. Details and the conditions that would flip the answer are below.

---

## Part 1 — Current cost profile (from the record)

### Domains — 16 total

Current state (renewal pricing), per the 2026-11-06 inventory
(`domain-inventory-and-streamlining-2026-11-06.md`, Gmail-notice-derived):

| Item | $/yr | Basis |
|---|---|---|
| phext.io (GoDaddy) | 77.99 | Renewed 2025-10-18 at $155.98 / 2 yr |
| mirrorborn.us (GoDaddy) | 19.99 | Auto-renews at $39.98 / 2 yr |
| Five 2024 GoDaddy names (sotafomo.com, quickfork.net, apertureshift.com, visionquest.me, wishnode.net) | ≥ 83.68 | Intro 3-yr pricing ($44.53–$60.97 each); **renewal prices are not in the mail record and are higher** — this is a floor |
| exectorox.com (Squarespace) | 22.00 | New rate from first renewal after 2026-12-06 |
| Six NFSN names (bickfordllamaranch.com, hb-aeromotive.com, xedoloh.com, askthewill.com, singularitywatch.org, snakefalls.org) | 82.94 | Actual auto-renewals: 4 × $14.19 (.com) + 2 × $13.09 (.org) |
| phextura.com + tluf.org (already at NFSN) | ≈ 27.28 | Not individually priced in the record; priced at NFSN's evidenced .com ($14.19) / .org ($13.09) renewal rates |
| **Total, current** | **≈ $312+/yr** | Inventory's own roll-up was $285+ for 14 domains; +2 NFSN names ≈ $312 |

Post-migration steady state, per the 2026-11-15 checklist
(`domain-migration-checklist-2026-11-15.md`) — this **corrects** the inventory's
~$190–200/yr estimate upward, because NFSN does not register or transfer .io or .me:

| Item | $/yr | Basis |
|---|---|---|
| Six transfers → NFSN (exectorox, sotafomo, apertureshift .com @ 14.19; quickfork, wishnode .net @ ~14.99; mirrorborn .us @ ~10.99) | ≈ 83.54 | .com at NFSN's evidenced renewal rate; .net/.us at NFSN transfer prices as proxy (their renewal prices are not in the record) |
| Eight already at NFSN (incl. phextura.com, tluf.org) | ≈ 110.22 | As above |
| phext.io — **stays at GoDaddy** (.io not offered at NFSN) | 77.99 | Unchanged |
| visionquest.me — **stays at GoDaddy** (.me not offered at NFSN) | ≥ 20.32 | **Renewal rate not in evidence**; floor is its intro rate ($60.97 / 3 yr). True figure will be higher |
| **Total, post-migration** | **≥ ≈ $292/yr** | One-time extras: $84.74 in transfer fees; ~$100 NFSN balance floor is a deposit, not a spend |

So the migration saves only ~$20/yr on evidenced numbers (more if the five unknown
GoDaddy renewals turn out steep). The domain bill is roughly **$292–312/yr in every
scenario below** — colocation changes nothing about it. It is also small next to hosting.

### Hosting — awsbox

- One hand-administered EC2 instance (44.248.235.76) serves everything: all the
  `/sites/web` static sites, edit.phext.io (phext-lattice app), mirrorborn.us.
- **Cost: ~$100/mo ≈ $1,200/yr — Will's own estimate (2026-11-15), not a billing pull.**
- No auto-scaling: a traffic spike cannot scale the instance cost (Will's own
  launch-eve bound, 2026-11-09). Exposure is egress, priced next.

### Bandwidth

No traffic logs are in the record; the properties are small static sites plus one
app, so assume low GB/mo. AWS egress at $0.09/GB, for scale:

| Egress / mo | Gross @ $0.09/GB | After AWS's 100 GB/mo free egress |
|---|---|---|
| 10 GB | $0.90 | $0 |
| 100 GB | $9.00 | $0 |
| 1,000 GB | $90.00 | $81.00 |

At any plausible current traffic, bandwidth is a rounding error inside the $100/mo.

### Other

- NFSN membership/hosting fees: **none in evidence** in the record — the NFSN names
  above are registration renewals only. Marked absent, not assumed zero-cost forever.

### Current profile total

| | Monthly | Annual |
|---|---|---|
| Hosting (AWS, Will's estimate) | $100 | $1,200 |
| Domains (current state) | ≈ $26 | ≈ $312 |
| **Total** | **≈ $126** | **≈ $1,512** |

---

## Part 2 — Lincoln / Nebraska alternatives (published prices, checked 2026-11-15)

### Colocation

**Binary.Net — The Vault / Sharp Annex, Lincoln, NE** (134 S 13th St / 206 S 13th St).
The one facility in Lincoln proper with published single-server pricing:

- **Single Server colocation (1U–4U): from $150/mo** — includes 24-hour monitoring,
  connectivity alerts, redundant fiber & power.
  Source: [binary.net/services](https://www.binary.net/services/)
- One-third rack (14U): from $400/mo, listed with unlimited data transfer.
  Source: [whtop Binary.Net review](https://www.whtop.com/review/binary.net)
- (Same facility also rents virtual servers from $50/mo — a VPS, not colo, noted
  because it is the only published Lincoln option under $100/mo.)

Market cross-checks for 1U-class colo in the metro:

- Lincoln and Omaha individual rack units "commonly run $95–175 a month."
  Sources: [internetaccess.org — Lincoln](https://www.internetaccess.org/server-colocation-nebraska-lincoln.html),
  [internetaccess.org — Omaha](https://www.internetaccess.org/server-colocation-nebraska-omaha.html)
- A broker band for Omaha 1–2U (1–3A, 1–5 TB) runs $285–475/mo — broker quotes,
  well above the published Binary.Net rate; treat as a ceiling, not a price.
  Source: [quotecolo.com — Nebraska](https://www.quotecolo.com/colocation-hosting/nebraska/)
- Other Nebraska facilities (1623 Farnam / Nebraska Data Centers and Pinpoint in
  Omaha, TierPoint in Papillion, Hamilton in Aurora/Doniphan/Grand Island) offer
  1U or single-server colo but publish **no price** — quote-only. None was assumed.

### Dedicated server rental (provider's hardware, Nebraska facility)

No Lincoln facility publishes a dedicated-rental price. Omaha does, and it is not
cheap:

- **HostColor, Omaha Edge dedicated: from $499/mo**, 1 Gbps+ unmetered ports,
  month-to-month default. Source: [vpscolor.com — Omaha Edge](https://www.vpscolor.com/dedicatedhosting/omaha-edge-dedicated-server.html)
- ServerMo, Omaha: cheapest listed configuration $724/mo (Xeon Silver 4210,
  128 GB — far more machine than these properties need, and priced like it).
  Source: [servermo.com — Omaha](https://www.servermo.com/dedicated-servers-usa/omaha/)
- Market summary for managed dedicated in Omaha: $200–600/mo.
  Source: [isplocators.com — Omaha managed hosting](https://www.isplocators.com/managed-hosting-nebraska-omaha.php)

### The DIY variant — a Lincoln site with fiber

ALLO's own FCC broadband labels for Lincoln addresses: **1 Gig ≈ $70–82/mo**
depending on rate card (Standard A $70, Standard B $75, Standard Plus $82),
unlimited data, no contract. A third-party read of the live Lincoln rate card
(Sept 2026) put 1 Gig at $108/mo.
Sources: [allocommunications.com — broadband labels](https://www.allocommunications.com/broadband-labels/?tier=five-standard-plus-crete-tab),
[myutilitysearch.com — ALLO Lincoln rate card](https://myutilitysearch.com/tag/best-fiber-internet/)

That prices the pipe only. The labels are consumer/small-business products — they
don't promise static IPs, an SLA, or server-hosting terms; business DIA is
quote-priced. Add hardware, power, and UPS, and the DIY site lands at parity with
AWS at best, with all operations on Will. Functionally it is the ranch pattern
relocated to a fiber-served address.

### Hardware (colo means bringing a machine)

- **$0 option:** colo an existing ranch mini PC. Caveat, honestly: Binary.Net's
  single-server plan is specified by height (1U–4U); a mini PC fits that envelope
  physically, but shelf mounting, power brick, and acceptance of non-rackmount
  gear are the facility's call and would need confirming. No rails, no redundant
  PSU, no out-of-band management.
- **Rackmount option:** refurbished Dell PowerEdge R450 1U (2× Xeon Gold 6330,
  192 GB — well beyond this workload) listed at **$3,237** at TechMikeNY.
  Source: [techmikeny.com — R450](https://techmikeny.com/products/dell-poweredge-r450-server-4-bay-lff-2x-gold-6330-2-00ghz-56-core-192gb-ram)
  Amortized: ≈ $90/mo over 3 years, ≈ $67/mo over 4 years. A right-sized
  prior-generation refurb would cost less; this is the concrete listing in hand.

---

## Part 3 — Verdict

Full web-property profile, domains (post-migration ≈ $292/yr) included in every column:

| Profile | Hosting / mo | Domains / mo | Total / mo | Total / yr | vs. current |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Current — AWS** (Will's ~$100/mo estimate) | $100 | ≈ $26 | **≈ $126** | **≈ $1,512** | — |
| **Colo — Binary.Net Lincoln** + existing mini PC ($0 hw) | $150 | ≈ $24 | ≈ $174 | ≈ $2,092 | **+$580/yr** |
| **Colo — Binary.Net Lincoln** + refurb 1U (amort. 4 yr) | $217 | ≈ $24 | ≈ $241 | ≈ $2,901 | +$1,389/yr |
| **Dedicated — HostColor Omaha** (cheapest published NE rental) | $499 | ≈ $24 | ≈ $523 | ≈ $6,280 | +$4,768/yr |

**On price, Lincoln colocation and dedicated hosting lose.** The cheapest real,
published, buyable Lincoln colo price (Binary.Net single server, $150/mo) is 50%
more than the entire AWS bill before hardware, and Nebraska dedicated rentals
start around $499/mo. The market-range floor for 1U colo ($95/mo) merely ties
AWS — and it is a range, not a published Lincoln price, and still excludes hardware.

**What would flip it:**

- AWS growing past ~$150/mo — a second instance, a much larger instance class,
  or paid add-ons — erases colo's premium at the mini-PC-hardware point.
- Sustained egress above ~1.7 TB/mo: at $0.09/GB, ~$150/mo of egress alone
  matches the colo rate, and colo plans bundle connectivity instead of metering it.
- Nothing on the domain side ever flips it: the ~$292/yr domain bill, including
  phext.io's $77.99/yr GoDaddy registration, is identical in every column.

**Non-price reasons that remain, stated plainly:** full ownership of the serving
hardware (the substrate test — owned outright, compounds); the public edge sits
in Lincoln, ~4 ms-class latency on ALLO's labels vs. a distant AWS region, and a
short drive for physical access at Binary.Net's Sharp Annex; no egress-fee
exposure at any traffic level; and one less dependency on AWS account standing.
Those are real. They are worth roughly $580/yr at the cheapest — that is the
honest price tag on them.

One footnote for completeness: if "Lincoln + fiber + cheaper than AWS" is ever
the goal in itself, Binary.Net's virtual servers in the same facility start at
$50/mo — but that is renting a slice of someone else's machine, the opposite of
the ownership argument for colo.

