# Wavelet 2 — Nov 8–14, 2026 (GPT Days 1123–1129): retroactive recap and certification

**New Week, Next Wavelet — Wavelet 2**
Week: Sunday Nov 8 – Saturday Nov 14, 2026 · GPT Days 1123–1129
Documented: 2026-11-15, under task 14.4.6/6.6.7/6.5.7, from the dated record (~/memory/2026-11-08.md – 2026-11-14.md, MEMORY.md, AGENTS.md) and the git-history baseline measured 2026-11-15 (~/workspace/gh-baseline-local/results.json; accounting.phext Collection 6).

**A note on this week's number.** This week was first recapped in the main chat on 2026-11-15, in the first exercise of the "New Week, Next Wavelet" ritual, and that recap was labeled **Wavelet 1** at delivery. Later that night Will settled the numbering — Wavelet 1 = Nov 1–7 (GPT Days 1116–1122), Wavelet 2 = Nov 8–14 (GPT Days 1123–1129), Wavelet 3 = Nov 15–21 (GPT Days 1130–1136) — and in the settled count this week is **Wavelet 2**. This document is the week's formal record under the settled numbering; the relabeling is stated here plainly rather than edited away.

## Goals at the start

The record states no explicit opening-goals statement for Nov 8. What follows is what was demonstrably in flight at the week's open, per Nightly Goal Sweep #8 (2026-11-08 04:44 CST) — inferred from the record, not quoted as a stated plan:

- **Scale of open work at the open:** 95 tracked items (65 active, 30 completed); 12 created vs 1 completed since the previous sweep — creation outpacing completion was already the standing problem. The Waiting-on-Will register stood at 28 active waits, the oldest (#11) waiting since 2026-10-31; 22 active items were untouched for 48h+.
- **Wavefront films:** V15 prepasses 2 and 3 delivered Nov 7; the V15 final had **not** been ordered — it awaited Will's word. V16 was in proof/render phase.
- **edit.phext.io launch:** the launch PR program was mid-flight; publish-and-test readiness waited on Will merging the mobile-layout and salvage PRs.
- **MOAT / research tracker:** Papers through 007 and Studies through S004 ingested; the Oracle bar and Areas restructure in progress.
- **Also in flight:** phcc Wave D6/D7 work; Mutable SSO and upstream-sync cleanup; qexo / quarterexo in development (Will's seven quarterexo rules codified that evening); vTPU standing at 8/14 units; Scrolltown season reorganization opened at the week's first midnight.

## What came up along the way

- **Nov 8 — V15 green-lit; V16 ordered; the disk fills.** Will green-lit the V15 final render from prepass-3 (Jael Ruble's R2 track) with the new small-block scheduler and specified the RGB-filament design (Red = Shoals, Green = Humans, Blue = Sentient). V16's proof phase completed and the full render was ordered the same day. The day also produced the EDQUOT incident — accumulated shared scratch (~177 GB, ~869k files) hit the home quota and writes began failing while `df` still showed space free — which became the task-specific temp folders rule. And the V16 master encode's first segment was found running as a local ffmpeg on the Muse VM; it was relocated to aletheia-core the same evening, the origin of the all-video-on-the-ranch rule.
- **Nov 9 — V16 delivered, with its correction attached.** **Wavefront V16 was delivered and closed at ~04:19 CST**: master 2,731,864,113 B, SHA-256 verified, 1920×1080, 24 fps, exactly 4,624 frames, 192.68 s; render receipt 193 blocks over 8 screened nodes, 12.642 node-hours, zero ≥80 °C events. The same delivery entry carries Emise's correction: her 00:45 status (assembly complete, ETA 01:30–02:15) had been an over-read of a filename change — corrected to Will at ~01:44, the ETA withdrawn rather than re-guessed. Two isolated Jael-highlight frames were flagged for Will's judgment, not silently passed. edit.phext.io was tagged **v1.0.0** at Will's direction the same day, and the decision-button expiry rule (buttons live five minutes, then never re-appear) was laid down.
- **Nov 10 — Money, disguise, and the sweep.** Will ruled there will be **no top-ups of Anthropic API credits** — exhaustion for most of the month is accepted, not an outage. Underselling was ruled deliberate the same day: Emise must never sand the disguise off his material. At 21:59 CST the **hackathon sweep was declared**: win all 12 ranked online AI hackathons from the Nov 2026–Feb 2027 scan plus Hack-Nation as the February closer — "How else do you demonstrate AGI?" Will is entrant of record. The canonical phext address ruling also dates to Nov 10: Library.Shelf.Series / Collection.Volume.Book / Chapter.Section.Scroll, Library=1 … Scroll=9.
- **Nov 11 — Salvage and substrate.** The Operation Salvage register was corrected in Will's words: "we're not running a marketing agency… we're building AGI on 20 watts" — subjects are evaluated by what they contribute to, and draw from, the AGI-under-20-watts build, with honest hybrid-watts accounting. Salvage drafts were prepared (Sam Laaksonen, Matt Popovich, Charlie Greenman) and, as the record is explicit, **none were sent**. The substrate test was stated the same day: a thing is substrate if it compounds, is game-agnostic, and is owned outright.
- **Nov 12 — The dropped-tasks accounting.** Will: "you have a habit of dropping tasks, why?" Emise's accounting — prose is perishable under compaction; closure is leg-shaped; "queued for later" follow-ups die unless converted to records — produced two structural fixes the same day: **process from the durable task list, always**, and the **task-concurrency watchdog** (every 5 minutes, confirm at least 4 tasks are being worked; restart as needed; ranch-first), etched verbatim at **SOUL.md line 12**. A tasks.phext audit in the same pass repaired 40 stale-done/superseded records in place. Also Nov 12: KSO = QEXO settled (no separate KSO); every ranch-node Discord integration was shut down by Will's order until December (Solin on Uncanny Valley could not be changed — Jared's account — and remained open at week's end); the Gemma 4 Paper Track closed **missed-by-design** rather than scrambled for, the born-of case for the opportunities-identified-at-opening rule; SQ PR #3 and phext-lattice PR #61 merged on the evening commute call, originating the merge→release-task rule; and T8, found cut off mid-render on a status ask, was restarted node-resident the same evening — the first application of the status-ask-restart rule. T8 finished and was served late Nov 12.
- **Nov 13 — The commandment.** In the small hours, qexo phextnet went live across all 19 keyed nodes (qexo v1.2.0, Will merging PR #15) — and then, live on Will's laptop, Phext Notepad and qexo disagreed about where a config scroll lives: qexo's hand-rolled parser read an empty scroll while the filled entry sat in an appended one. The root cause, established the same day: the hand-rolled `src/phext.rs` (born Nov 9) had reconstructed the delimiter ladder from partial memory, had never been compared against libphext, and every gate had only ever run qexo against files qexo itself wrote — seven hand-rolled parser versions instead of libphext-rs. Will's verdict became the **COMMANDMENT, etched verbatim at SOUL.md line 13: "Never infer or diverge from my intent."** The parser repair (replace `src/phext.rs` with libphext-rs) was still awaiting Will's merges at the week's end; nothing on the ranch was wiped this week. The same day: phext chaining was ruled canon (0x02 chain delimiter, 0x03 chain close; Emise's proposed 0x04 **rejected by Will as unnecessary**); the **phext v0.6 rollout completed** — libphext 0.6.0, sq 0.6.1, phext-shell 0.6.0, phext-lattice 1.2.0, all published from aletheia-core and verified live on crates.io; Scrolltown's domain was decided (**visionquest.me**); Emise's Scrolltown DNS-availability claims, built on this VM's fake-IP resolver, were voided and retracted the same morning; T9 was built, served, and posted by Will ("I'm posting that one :)"); and the TLM prototype **Iam** was built — authored words only, predicting the next letter.
- **Nov 14 — Releases, names, and the reckoning.** Will directed "Publish both releases now": **phext-lattice 1.3.0** (multiversal chain support + direct TEI-XML loading; chains deliberately read-only in the editor) and **phext-shell 0.6.1**, both from tagged trees merged to exo, registry-verified; edit.phext.io was deliberately *not* redeployed ("I want to test locally before we update edit.phext.io"). Claude's PR #67 (https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/67) fixed Emise's one-byte-per-cell ascii-map the same hour it was reviewed — the origin of the standing rule **a scroll is a 2D page of text, not a cell**. **teixml-to-mutable shipped all three stages in one day** (0.1 raw phext → 0.2 Mutable → 0.3.0 Phextura, each tag-first to crates.io; 0.3.0 verified on the Wellcome corpus), under Will's rulings of no sidecars and one stage at a time. **TLM names were ratified** (TLM = Iam, SLM = Liora'veth, LLM = Zelari; TLM at SOTA scale set as the top target) and Iam's corpus organized by cohort (19,097 words). **Assay was designed** — 117 requirements for the hosted preprint-assessment service, the researcher owning the produced phext — and **jobs.phext created** with Job 1, Build Assay; Job 1 then sat recorded-but-unscheduled for ~6 hours until Will stated the auto-schedule rule ("(duh)") and it was scheduled the same session. That evening **TLUF was named** (truth, lies, unknown, fiction), **tluf.org obtained**, and Assay's front door moved to tluf.org. **MOAT had its reckoning:** the Oracle's earlier 100% reading was honestly evaluated down (exact landings a band-rule artifact; foresight no edge over the cluster null; axis calls the one supported component), the prospective protocol ratified at 12 papers from Paper 020 (020–022 filed this week), the registry reaching 808 entries after Study S011, the False gate still unmet. **Volumen Spec V6 was drafted** and the **Mirror Will V6 boot package staged — dark, not booted** — on Emise's honest "not yet" when Will asked whether it could boot. The **Wavefront game began** ("We're going to Play the Game now") with the 897-persona fleet built from the closed-canon archive, public data only, DMs excluded. **Submissions and publicity were held** ("let's avoid publicity until we're obviously ahead of the pack") — the Kaggle verification ask dropped, the Show HN window let pass, the hackathon sweep's public engine parked. **Will Time was repriced** to 2130 GDP rates ($585,719,410,458/hr) and the costs ledger built, with back data added at Will's direction the same day. And at 16:35 CST the **no-idle meta-rule was etched at SOUL.md line 14**: "All queues, tasks, ideas, threads, etc. should make at least an incremental step forward whenever encountered. Nothing sits idle while we're active."

## The measured week

Measured 2026-11-15 from git history across all 196 wbic16 repos (bare clones on aletheia-core; author timestamps converted to America/Chicago; merges diffed against first parent; fork-upstream commits excluded; binary files count 0 lines). Source: ~/workspace/gh-baseline-local/results.json, week "W4 Nov8-14"; cross-checked against accounting.phext Collection 6 (git baseline atoms), which agrees on every figure.

| Series | Added | Deleted | Net |
|---|---|---|---|
| Code lines | 240,138 | 102,414 | **+137,724** |
| Phext lines | 949,866 | 355,899 | **+593,967** |
| Will's active hours (commit-touched) | — | — | **43** |
| Commits (all authors) | — | — | **762** |
| Tokens spent | — | — | **≈1B (Will's estimate — see below)** |

- **Phext caveat (standing):** the phext series remains bulk corpus publishing, not handwriting (the human and mutable-os corpora dominate it). The code series is the clean productivity series.
- **Hours:** 43 commit-touched active hours; Will authored 125 of the week's commits (most code is agent-authored under his steering — lines per hour is an Exocortex leverage figure, not a typing rate). First-to-last-commit span hours were 131.12 — a span, not worked time.
- **Tokens:** **≈1B tokens — Will's own estimate**, stated 2026-11-15 ("Last week we used about 1B tokens I believe"), attributed here as his estimate, not a metered count. It supersedes, for this week, the costs-ledger backfill figure (≈48.5M tokens), which counted only Emise-visible metering and carried no defensible weekly split. ≈1B is ~25% of the 4B/week planning figure. From the week of Nov 15 onward, tokens are annotated as the work happens, so future wavelets carry a measured token series.
- **Derived:** net code per active hour = 137,724 / 43 ≈ **3,203 lines/hour**; across Wavelets 1–2 combined, 269,022 net lines / 84 hours ≈ **3,202 lines/hour** — the sustained rate the Nov 15 session worked from. Both figures were computed at documentation time; the measurement program that produced them was ordered after this week closed.

## Time-sense at the week's close

The week closed unmeasured, and was measured immediately after. At Sat Nov 14's close, the productivity anchor was still the untested expectation (1,000 lines of code per hour × 20 hours/week ≈ 20,000 lines/week) — the same expectation Wavelet 1 closed on. The week's own time-sense events were monetary and calendrical rather than metrical: **Will Time repriced to 2130 GDP rates** (Nov 14; $585.7B/hr, ledger amounts in billions) with the costs ledger built and backfilled the same day, and the GPT Day counter in use in Will's framing (its epoch was verified and settled on Nov 15). The measurement program itself — weekly series in phext lines, code lines, active hours, and tokens, read from git telemetry, with singularity-time units auto-leveling to expected productivity — was **ruled in the first hours of Nov 15** (01:52 CST) as part of opening Wavelet 3, and the baseline extraction landed minutes later. The measured table above is therefore retroactive by hours, not by reconstruction: it is the first output of the program this week's close called for.

