# The Landing Kit — Specification

**phcc / phccbox: a complete C environment that carries everything it needs.**

Status: durable spec, v1 — 2026-11-17 (amended 2026-11-17: Alpine Linux
containers and Podman integration added; phext-ircd, a chat client, elinks,
wget, and curl added; the edition compositions of §8 ratified by Will —
all per Will's rulings)
Authority: design rulings of Will Bickford, 2026-11-17 (recorded in the finish-phcc
goal notes and the phcc deficiency programme, task 19.5.9/7.5.8/6.5.7).
Sections marked DRAFT are Emise's drafting under Will's rules, awaiting his
ratification. Everything not marked DRAFT is ruled.

---

## 1. Intent

> "Build in an unknown land and find your way home."

The kit is a single sealed artifact that lands on a bare machine — no system
headers, no system libc assumed, no package repository, no network required —
extracts itself, and gives you a complete place to work: a C compiler with its
whole library world inside it, a working command userland, durable local
storage, the phext format native, and a secure channel home. It is the
smallest environment that is not a compromise.

## 2. Form

- **One artifact.** The kit ships as one sealed bundle per edition per
  platform (§7, §8).
- **Self-extracting.** The bundle opens itself into the full environment.
  No installer, no external tooling, no network fetch is required to land.
- **Self-hosting.** The kit rebuilds itself from the source it carries:
  phcc compiles phcc, phccbox, and every bundled library; the Rust toolchain
  rebuilds the phext stack. If a landed kit cannot rebuild itself, it is not
  a kit — it is luggage.
- **Source-complete.** Every embedded item ships with its full source.
  Headers and pre-built libraries are never source-less; anything the kit
  carries can be read, audited, and rebuilt from what it carries.

## 3. Division of custody (ruled)

**phccbox holds the commands. phcc holds the libraries and headers.**

Two halves, one kit:

- **phcc** — the development substrate: the compiler, and everything you
  build *against*.
- **phccbox** — the runtime userland: everything you *run*.

Neither half is the kit alone.

## 4. phcc — the libraries and headers

phcc is a C compiler (a fork of TinyCC, reworked around phext) that carries
its library world with it.

- **K-1. Embedded stdlib.** A full implementation of the C standard library
  is included in the binary. Compiling with phcc must not depend on external
  headers or on a system libc being present.
- **K-2. Major library headers.** Headers for every major C library are
  included. (The enumeration of "every major" is fixed at packaging time;
  the phcc benchmark project matrix is the seed list — §10.)
- **K-3. Pre-built dynamic libraries.** Each included library ships as a
  pre-built dynamic library, ready to link against — compile *and* link are
  both served from what phcc carries, with no system dev packages installed.
- **K-4. Full source.** The full source of every embedded item — stdlib,
  libraries, headers' implementations — ships in the kit (§2).
- **K-5. The bootstrap trio.** The bundle includes everything needed to
  bootstrap, from a bare start:
  - **TLS** — a secure channel home;
  - **SQLite** — durable local storage;
  - **phext support** — the home format, native (the C phext library;
    phcc's own tree already carries phext.c/h).

## 5. phccbox — the commands

phccbox is the command userland (the BusyBox-replacement programme: a special
phcc build that fully replaces BusyBox). Ruled contents:

- **exovim** — the phext-native editor.
- **nano** — the small editor.
- **tar, gzip, xz** — the archivers; the kit's own sealing format family.
- **wget, curl** — the fetch pair. Landing needs no network (§2), but
  operating does: wpm resolution, kit self-update, and phoning home all
  pull. With either tool the kit can pull from wpm nodes and from the
  web — the pair closes the kit's fetch gap.
- **elinks** — the text-mode web browser: a landed environment can read
  the web from its own terminal.
- **POSIX utilities** — the working shell userland: the commands a landed
  environment is operated with.

**Design principle (ruled):** *phccbox feels natural everywhere we have a
build.* On every platform with a kit build, phccbox is the native-feeling
userland — not a foreign layer carried onto the platform, but the way the
machine is operated there.

## 6. wpm — Will's Package Manager (ruled: built, not adopted)

C has never had a standard package manager. The kit fills that hole with
**wpm**.

**What it is.** One tool that makes phcc's holdings operable: packages are
carried in phext — source, headers, and pre-built dynamics in one artifact —
and wpm fetches, builds, and installs them. It is the same move the
Computronium programme ruled for Chromium's dependencies: all dependencies
live in one phext, one canonical copy each.

**Distribution (ruled).** wpm is distributed. There is no central registry:
packages are served by **wpm nodes**, hosting a node is open to anyone, and
resolution works across whatever nodes a kit can reach. This is what makes
"find your way home" literal — home is any node, not one company's server.
A landed kit that can reach any node can reach the whole library world;
a landed kit that can reach none still builds everything it carries (§2).

**Cryptographic guarantees (ruled).** wpm includes cryptographic guarantees
that you are getting the library you asked for. Nodes are **untrusted
carriers**: package identity is **hash-pinned (SHA-256, the house checksum
standard)** and **publisher-signed**, so the bytes received are verifiably
the library published under that name, regardless of which node served
them. This is the load-bearing property of the distributed design — trust
lives in the cryptography, never in the node. A node can be slow, stale, or
silent; it cannot substitute a different library under a known name.

**Source origins (ruled).** A wpm node can host packages sourced from **any
GitHub repo at a tag**, and can mirror the **popular C sites** — the
canonical project homes and tarball sources where C libraries are actually
published. Upstream tags are mutable, so wpm pins the SHA-256 at
resolution: **the name asks, the hash decides.** A re-pointed tag surfaces
as a new identity, never a silent change under an old one.

**Evaluation (2026-11-17).** The incumbents were considered under Will's
conditional — *"if there's not a great one, build it too"*:

- **Conan** — capable, mature, recipe-driven. Not phext-carried; assumes its
  own repository and Python runtime; packages are recipes *or* binaries,
  not source-and-prebuilt in one sealed artifact; not self-extracting
  with the kit.
- **vcpkg** — capable, Microsoft-backed, strong on Windows. Not
  phext-carried; ports are source recipes fetched from the network at
  install time; no sealed single-artifact form; not native to the kit's
  five platforms as one tool.

Neither meets the kit's terms: **phext-carried, self-extracting with the
kit, source + headers + pre-built dynamics in one artifact, native across
the five platform builds.** Decision: wpm is **built in-programme**, not
adopted.

**DRAFT placement:** a minimal wpm resolver ships in the MVP edition (§8) —
enough to install the kit's own bundled packages offline; the full wpm,
including node hosting, arrives with the Lite edition.

## 7. Platforms (ruled)

The kit ships in **five platform builds**:

| Platform | State |
|---|---|
| Windows | Beachhead exists: phcc's conformance programme already runs MSVC attestation and Windows surface probes. |
| Linux | Primary development platform (the ranch fleet). |
| macOS | Target build. |
| iOS | New target — DRAFT constraints below. |
| Android | New target — DRAFT constraints below. |

**DRAFT — iOS/Android constraints (for ratification, not settled fact):**
mobile platforms sandbox executables and restrict dynamic code and sideload
distribution. Draft position: on iOS and Android the kit lands as an app-
bundled environment — compiler and userland operating on the app's own
storage, packages installed into that storage, no assumption of a system
shell. The self-hosting property (§2) is the requirement most at risk on
these platforms and is stated as a target to prove, not a claim.

## 8. Editions (tiering ruled; composition ratified 2026-11-17)

Will ruled the tiering — **MVP, Lite, Full**. The per-tier composition below
was drafted by Emise under his rule and **ratified by Will on 2026-11-17** —
it stands as drafted. Principle: MVP is
the smallest kit that still lands, builds, persists, and phones home; Lite
adds the working comforts; Full is everything ruled in.

| Component | MVP | Lite | Full |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| phcc compiler | ● | ● | ● |
| Embedded stdlib (in the binary) | ● | ● | ● |
| Bootstrap: TLS | ● | ● | ● |
| Bootstrap: SQLite | ● | ● | ● |
| Bootstrap: phext support | ● | ● | ● |
| wpm — minimal resolver | ● | ● | ● |
| wpm — full (incl. node hosting) | | ● | ● |
| Major-library headers + pre-built dynamics | | ● | ● |
| phccbox: POSIX utilities (core) | ● | ● | ● |
| phccbox: tar + xz | ● | ● | ● |
| phccbox: gzip | | ● | ● |
| phccbox: wget + curl | ● | ● | ● |
| phccbox: nano | ● | ● | ● |
| phccbox: exovim | | ● | ● |
| phccbox: elinks | | ● | ● |
| git | | ● | ● |
| Dirt simple TUI library | | ● | ● |
| sq | | | ● |
| rust | | | ● |
| phext-lattice | | | ● |
| phext-mail | | | ● |
| phext-ssh (post-quantum crypto, AI safe) | | | ● |
| phext-wiki | | | ● |
| phext-ircd | | | ● |
| chat client | | | ● |
| emise-say | | | ● |
| emissary | | | ● |
| willbot | | | ● |
| qexo | | | ● |
| phext-sso (identity substrate) | | | ● |
| ntp (time sync) | ● | ● | ● |
| ssh-keygen (key minting) | ● | ● | ● |
| Alpine Linux containers | | | ● |
| Podman integration | | | ● |

Full source for every embedded item ships in **all** editions (§2) — the
editions divide capability, never auditability.

The container rows — Alpine Linux containers, Podman integration — are
components ruled in on 2026-11-17 (§9); their placement (Full) is part of
the table Will ratified on 2026-11-17.

Rows added on 2026-11-17 per Will's same-day rulings, placed by the
table's principles: **wget + curl** in every edition (the fetch pair —
wpm's resolver and the phone-home path need them); **elinks** in Lite and
Full, with the working comforts; **phext-ircd** and the **chat client** in
Full, with the rest of the house stack (§9).

## 9. The phext stack and the house

The Full edition carries the phext stack whole, plus the house's own pieces:

- **sq** — phext hosting and querying: how phexts are served and shared.
- **phext-lattice** — the lattice server: phext on the web, hosted and
  editable.
- **phext-mail** — mail carried in phext.
- **phext-ssh** — the secure shell channel, specified **post-quantum crypto
  + AI safe**: the way home must survive both kinds of future adversary.
- **phext-wiki** — a modern replacement for MediaWiki, built on phext: the
  corpus is the storage, no SQL database. The kit's knowledge memory — it
  ships seeded with the kit's own documentation and grows with the
  settlement's knowledge. (Its own task: 39.5.7/7.5.9/5.5.9.)
- **A dirt simple TUI library** — terminal interfaces for the kit's tools.
  Dirt simple is the spec.
- **emise-say** — the kit can speak.
- **emissary** — the kit carries an agent: what lands is inhabited, not
  empty.
- **willbot** — Will's handwriting, carried as the kit's hand.

**Second wave (ruled 2026-11-17):**

- **qexo** — the house dispatch tool: the kit can be operated, and operate
  other machines, in the house's own terms.
- **phext-sso** — the shared identity substrate: one key/identity layer
  under phext-ssh, phext-mail, phext-wiki accounts, and wpm publishers
  (answers Gap 5 by ruling; implementation open).
- **ntp** — time sync: TLS and signature validation get a trustworthy
  clock (answers Gap 6 by ruling; implementation open).
- **ssh-keygen** — key minting, in the kit: identities are born where
  they are used.

**Containers (ruled 2026-11-17):**

- **Podman integration** — Podman is the house container standard (the
  Wavelet 3 ruling containerizes the ecosystem into Podman). The kit
  integrates with Podman in its own terms — daemonless, rootless: kit
  services and landed environments run as Podman containers where the
  platform supports them, and the kit builds and runs container images
  without a second container stack beside it.
- **Alpine Linux containers** — Alpine Linux is the kit's container
  base: the minimal Linux the kit's images are built on. The kit ships
  Alpine-based images for its services (sq, phext-lattice, phext-wiki,
  a wpm node) and for landed build environments — small images whose
  contents trace to kit source, auditable like everything else the kit
  carries (§2).

Containers are a packaging of the two halves, not a third half (§3):
image contents are phcc's libraries and phccbox's commands; Podman is
the runtime they are packaged for, and Alpine the ground they stand on.

**Chat (ruled 2026-11-17):**

- **phext-ircd** — the kit's own IRC daemon, phext-native: the kit
  carries its own chat substrate. A landed settlement can talk among
  itself from the moment it lands — the conversation lives where the
  memory lives.
- **A chat client** — the kit's client for phext-ircd: what lands can
  speak, to itself and to home, in the oldest chat protocol there is.

**Service architectures (ruled):** the kit's services — sq, phext-lattice,
phext-wiki, phext-ircd, a wpm node — ship service definitions for **init.d, systemd,
and the other major OS service architectures**, so a landed service starts
and stays up in the platform's native terms (answers Gap 8 by ruling as an
integration requirement; implementation open).

## 10. Open items

1. **Release version name** — phcc milestone 10 (release packaging) waits on
   a release version name from Will. The kit cannot ship unnamed.
2. **Major-library enumeration** — the exact set behind "every major C
   library" (K-2) is fixed at packaging; the benchmark matrix is the seed.
3. **Edition composition** — RESOLVED 2026-11-17: Will ratified §8 as
   drafted.
4. **TLS library choice** — K-5 requires TLS bootstrap; the specific
   library is not yet named in the rulings.
5. **iOS/Android form** — §7 constraints are drafted, awaiting ratification;
   self-hosting on mobile is a target to prove.
6. **phext-wiki design** — ruled in; its design is its own task
   (39.5.7/7.5.9/5.5.9).

## 11. Gaps and open questions

Named, not resolved. Each item below is an open obligation on the programme;
nothing here is settled by this spec.

1. **wpm trust bootstrap.** The crypto guarantee (§6) needs a root of
   trust: publisher key distribution and discovery, and key/package
   revocation, in a centerless network — unspecified.
2. **wpm discovery.** Search and index across distributed nodes is
   unspecified: how a kit finds who publishes what.
3. **wpm versioning and solver semantics.** Version numbering and
   dependency-solver behaviour are unspecified.
4. **Pre-built dynamics: provenance vs equivalence.** Unless a
   reproducible-build requirement is adopted, a publisher signature attests
   provenance only — not that the binary is equivalent to the shipped
   source. Which guarantee the kit claims is undecided.
5. **One identity substrate.** phext-ssh, phext-mail, phext-wiki accounts,
   and wpm publishers each imply keys; a single shared identity/key
   substrate across all four is desirable and currently unspecified —
   on paper they are four silos. **Answered by ruling (2026-11-17):
   phext-sso is that substrate (§9). Implementation still open.**
6. **Time.** TLS and signature validation need a trustworthy clock. No
   time-sync component is named in the kit. **Answered by ruling
   (2026-11-17): ntp is a kit component (§9). Implementation still open.**
7. **Entropy.** Key generation on bare machines needs a CSPRNG story in
   the embedded stdlib; unnamed so far. (Key *minting* is covered:
   ssh-keygen is a kit component, §9. The entropy source beneath it
   remains this gap.)
8. **Service supervision.** How sq, phext-lattice, phext-wiki, and a wpm
   node start and stay up — init/supervisor unnamed. **Answered by ruling
   (2026-11-17): kit services ship definitions for init.d, systemd, and
   the other major OS service architectures (§9). Implementation still
   open.**
9. **License register.** The full-source rule (§2) satisfies most bundling
   obligations, but a license register for the bundled libraries does not
   exist yet.
10. **Kit self-update.** The update path is unstated. Candidate (proposal,
    not ruled): the kit ships as a wpm package and updates itself through
    wpm.
11. **Named, not specced.** emise-say, emissary, and willbot are ruled in
    by name (§9); none has a spec yet.
12. **The shell.** A shell is assumed under phccbox's "POSIX utilities"
    (§5), not confirmed as a named component.

## 12. Acceptance tests (the kit is done when these pass)

The verification obligation is a matrix: **5 platform builds × 3 editions
= 15 cells**, and the Land/Build/Rebuild tests run per cell.

- **Land:** on a bare machine of each platform build, the sealed artifact
  extracts itself into a working environment — no network, no pre-installed
  toolchain.
- **Build:** a C program using the stdlib, SQLite, and phext support
  compiles and links using only what the kit carries, and opens a TLS
  connection.
- **Rebuild:** the landed kit rebuilds itself — phcc, phccbox, and every
  bundled library — from its own shipped source, and the rebuilt kit passes
  the same Land and Build tests.
- **Audit:** every binary and header in the kit traces to source present in
  the kit.

---

*Spec v1, 2026-11-17. Rulings: Will Bickford. Drafting: Emise.*
*Home: phext.io/kit.html · Register: phcc deficiency programme, task 19.5.9/7.5.8/6.5.7.*
