# qexo dispatch core — SPEC (RATIFIED 2026-11-16)

Task: 26.5.9/7.6.11/5.5.10 · Drafted 2026-11-16 (Emise, qexo chat)
Status: RATIFIED 2026-11-16 — Will answered all six §15 open
questions OBO in the qexo chat (2026-11-16 02:21–02:23 CST):
Q1, Q3, Q4, Q5, Q6 ratified as recommended; Q2 decided AGAINST
the draft's recommendation — the §10.4 service token is widened
to resolutions + entries-append (§§10.4, 10.5, and the §13
slice-3 gates amended at close-out to the ratified scope).
Build slices follow in order.
Base tree for every build slice below: wbic16/quarterexo exo @
609bab65 (v1.7.1), synced to wbic16/qexo per the two-repo rule
(task 29.6.7/6.5.7/6.5.8).

Amendments after ratification:
- 2026-11-16 (Will, main chat, 20:30 CST) — FAIR DISPATCH:
  "you need a round-robin scheduler - don't always focus on
  using aletheia-core" / "use qexo to dispatch fairly - random
  selection per round". Scope: execution target selection
  ONLY — §6 amended (random draw per round from the eligible
  set, receipted), slice-4 gates added in §13.3, fairness
  hooks added in §14. The §15 ratifications stand untouched:
  aletheia-core keeps its drainer/coordinator and signer
  roles; it loses only an execution preference it should
  never have had. Conflict check against slice 1 as built
  (quarterexo PR #29, branch emise/dispatch-slice1-chain-state
  @ bcaa304f): NO CONFLICT — slice 1 contains no placement,
  target-selection, or default-node logic. Its dispatch.rs is
  local-only enqueue/queue/show with no network or execution
  primitives; the only node name in it is the `enqueued-by`
  provenance recorded on an item (and its test fixture), and
  `queue --node` filters by that provenance — who enqueued an
  item, never where it runs. Placement is slice 4's to build,
  now under this ruling.

## 0. What this is, and the layering

Will's mission for qexo, verbatim (2026-11-14 01:37 CST): "qexo
should do 3 things, very well: 1. prioritize immediate action
2. focus on the highest ROI actions 3. ensure nothing I've queued
is ever lost again" — with "qexo needs to be multiversal phext
aware and native" and "we can use it to dispatch work to the
entire cluster efficiently."

Will's layering, verbatim (2026-11-16 01:08 CST): "have qexo
integrate with register.phext.io and then ship it - I'll switch
to CLI first and use my phone and that mostly for dispatch" —
and: "we can use the muse.ai channels for planning and
discussion."

So the three layers, stated once and used everywhere below:

- **Planning/discussion layer — the muse.ai chats.** Nothing in
  this spec moves them. Decisions are made in conversation, as
  today.
- **Shared durable queue — the register** (register.phext.io,
  repo wbic16/register). The never-lost queue made visible:
  numbered items, permanent monotonic numbers, quick entries in,
  resolutions out.
- **Dispatch/execution layer — qexo.** Nodes hold execution
  state: queue items, attempts, heartbeats, checkpoints,
  receipts, audits, bridges. qexo is the register's drainer
  (§10) and the fleet's dispatcher (§§3–9).

Ratified feature set this spec implements: the three mission
properties; multiversal-native state; cluster dispatch;
F4 auto-verification with an audit view (Will's amendment,
2026-11-14 01:42 CST); F5 self-resuming tasks, auto-pause after
3 failures (Will's amendment, same minute); F6 stable bridges
to all inference sources (Will's addition, same minute). The
register integration (§10) and the surface priorities (§2.3)
are Will's binding amendment of 2026-11-16 01:08 CST.

One seam, named not absorbed: artifact *deployment* (multi-
artifact release manifests, service installs) is PH:
DevSecOps' standard (task 10.4.4/5.4.4/4.4.5, DRAFT,
unratified). Dispatch bundles (§3.4) are work orders, not
deploy artifacts; the two share only the signer trust and the
serve transport. This spec does not design deploy manifests.

## 1. Mission mapping — property → mechanism → test

**P1. Prioritize immediate action.**
Mechanism: enqueue is write-before-ack on the local node
(§4.1) — no network round trip stands between intent and a
durable queue record. Dispatch of an already-queued item is a
single signed bundle POST (§3.4). Everything a dispatch needs
is pre-staged: peer endpoints and keys are already in state
(shipped `register`), bridge liveness is pre-probed on a
cadence (§9), placement inputs (capability, temperature) are
already telemetry (8.8.8 homeland, shipped). The latency
budget: enqueue→ack ≤ 1 s on the node itself; enqueue→first
action (bundle accepted by the executing node, acceptance
receipt written) ≤ 10 s on a healthy tailnet. Both numbers
are measured from record timestamps, never estimated (§14).
Test: the bench pack (§14) measures both on every change.

**P2. Focus on the highest-ROI actions.**
Mechanism: the queue's order is *calculated* from fields on
each item record — daily-goal impact class first, then the
ROI ratio (value ÷ cost, both recorded on the record), then
age, oldest first (§5). Order is never a stored list, so it
cannot drift from the records. `qexo queue` prints the
calculated order with each item's sort keys visible;
`qexo queue --explain <item>` shows the keys and every audit
record that ever changed them. Re-ordering is a field edit
plus an audit record — the ordering itself is auditable.
Test: fixture queues with known fields must render in the
calculated order; every re-order in the fixture leaves its
audit.

**P3. Ensure nothing queued is ever lost.**
Mechanism: five obligations, each checkable (§4): write-
before-ack enqueue; every state transition and its receipt
land in the same state save; terminal states exist only with
a receipt and live-surface evidence; no verb deletes a queue
record (retirement is a status line); `qexo doctor` runs a
queue-integrity reconciliation whose counts are calculated
from the records. Register-born items have a second durable
copy of their identity (the register itself, §10), and every
item cites its tasks.phext coordinate, a third.
Test: the integrity reconciliation must report zero lost on
every fixture, including crash-mid-save and wipe/rebirth
fixtures (§14).

## 2. Verbs

Style follows the shipped surface (quarterexo @ 609bab65,
src/cli.rs): lowercase single-word verbs, positional arguments,
`--check` for the byte-inert dry run (update/evolve precedent),
`--fix` for repair (doctor precedent), flags for setters
(config precedent). No new `--json`: machine readers read
phext (`qexo select`/`get`), humans read the renderings —
the one shipped exception, `list --json`, is not extended.

### 2.1 The dispatch verb set

- `qexo enqueue <brief-file>` — file a queue item from a
  brief scroll (§3.2). Flags: `--title <t>`,
  `--coordinate <task-coordinate>`, `--register <n>`,
  `--value <1-9>`, `--cost <1-9>`, `--goal <text>`,
  `--after <item-address>` (dependency: do not dispatch
  until that item is done). Prints the item's full 9D
  address — only after the state save completes (§4.1).
  `qexo enqueue -` reads the brief from stdin.
- `qexo queue [--all] [--node <name>]` — the calculated
  order (§5), human rendering: state, title, sort keys,
  age, failures. `--all` includes terminal items. Paused-
  auto items (§8) pin to the head, flagged, always.
- `qexo queue --explain <item-address>` — the item's sort
  keys, its calculated position, and its re-order audit
  trail.
- `qexo dispatch [<item-address>] [--to <node>] [--check]`
  — dispatch the head item, or the named item, to the
  placed node (§6) or the named node. `--check` prints the
  placement decision and the bundle hash; writes nothing.
- `qexo show <address>` — render one record. The address
  may carry a universe prefix: `qexo show
  $2:11.11.11/2.1.1/1.1.1` reads attempt 2's copy (§3.3).
  (select/get remain the raw file ops; show is the
  dispatch-aware reader.)
- `qexo pause <item-address> [--note <text>]` — manual
  pause; dispatch skips it until resumed.
- `qexo resume <item-address>` — return a paused item to
  queued; also the manual override of an auto-pause
  (resets the consecutive-failure counter, §8, and writes
  the audit that says who overrode).
- `qexo cancel <item-address> --note <text>` — terminal,
  Will/Emise only, note required. For a register-born
  item this is the act that writes the register
  resolution (§10.3).
- `qexo audit [--item <address>] [--kind <kind>]
  [--since <date>]` — F4's view (§7): audit records,
  newest last (ledger order). `qexo audit show <address>`
  renders one in full.
- `qexo bridge` — list bridges with calculated health.
  `qexo bridge --add <name> --kind <kind> --endpoint
  <url> [--capability <text>]` (repeatable capability),
  `qexo bridge --check [<name>]` (probe now, append
  health evidence), `qexo bridge --retire <name>`.
  Flag style follows `config --release-signer`.
- `qexo drain [--check]` — the register drain (§10):
  pull pending entries + resolutions, file/update queue
  items, advance watermarks. `--check` reports what a
  drain would do; writes nothing.
- `serve` gains one route: `POST /v1/dispatch` (§3.4).
  The shipped GET routes are untouched.

### 2.2 What does not change

Bare `qexo` still renders the plan. `evolve` keeps its
gossip/persona/update meaning; dispatch does not overload
it. `update` keeps the release trust rule exactly as
shipped (assess_offer: signer → signature → platform →
version → download, per PR #27's line).

### 2.3 Will's surfaces — CLI first, phone main (binding)

Per the 2026-11-16 amendment: Will dispatches from the
**CLI first**, and **the Android app is his main dispatch
surface** ("use my phone and that mostly for dispatch").

- Every verb in §2.1 has an `--app` counterpart speaking
  the shipped learn `--app` line protocol (src/learn.rs:
  one command per line; replies are `key: value` blocks
  closed by `end: <command>`; greeting block at start;
  NOT JSON). `enqueue --app`, `queue --app`,
  `dispatch --app`, `show --app`, `pause --app`,
  `resume --app`, `audit --app`, `drain --app` — each a
  second driver over the same state machine, the exact
  pattern `learn --app` set against the learn TUI.
- Launcher quick actions (wbic16/qexo, launcher v3):
  **Enqueue** (one text field + brief paste → enqueue),
  **Queue** (the calculated order at phone width; tap an
  item → its show view), **Dispatch now** (head item),
  **Pause / Resume** buttons on the item view, **Audit**
  (recent audits, phone width). Birth/config stays as
  shipped.
- Two-repo sync (standing rule, task 29.6.7/6.5.7/
  6.5.8): every dispatch feature lands in quarterexo
  and wbic16/qexo as the same surface in the same
  release train; Android-specific deltas (the launcher,
  the state-path fallback, the timer truth) are the
  only sanctioned differences, preserved through syncs.

## 3. State model — multiversal, phext-native, no JSON

Internal representation is scrolls and coordinates only
(DESIGN.md §1, shipped). JSON exists in exactly two
places in this design, both foreign edges, both named:
the register service's HTTP API (§10 — their wire, our
translation at the edge, the infer/Ollama precedent) and
the Ollama edge itself (unchanged). No JSON value crosses
into a state scroll, a bundle, or a receipt.

### 3.1 A new homeland: dispatch — 11.11.11

LAYER2's homelands run 1.1.1–9.9.9 plus lessons at
10.10.10 (v1.4.0). Dispatch takes the next patterned
anchor, proposed here for ratification by use, exactly
as LAYER2 prescribes ("first placements, not a schema"):

- **11.11.11/11.11.11/11.11.11 — dispatch & queue** (the
  elevens: send-off country). Index scroll at the anchor,
  prose map like every homeland index. Clusters
  (Collection) inside it, following the shipped lattice
  convention (Collection 1 = the index's own vicinity;
  families from Collection 2):
  - C2 — **queue items**: one Book per item, Books in
    arrival sequence (a ledger family — the local
    sequence is the point, the releases cluster's
    precedent). Scroll 1 = the item record (§3.2);
    Scroll 2 = the brief text; Scroll 3+ = dependency
    and context citations in prose.
  - C3 — **assignments**: one Book per live assignment
    (item address, executing node, attempt number,
    bundle hash, last heartbeat, last checkpoint cite).
    An assignment Book retires (status line) when its
    attempt closes.
  - C4 — **schedules**: one Book per scheduled entry
    (§13, cutover C3): cadence text, item template,
    last-fired cite.
  - C5 — **bridges**: one Book per bridge (§9). Scroll 1
    = the bridge record; Scroll 2+ = the health log,
    one scroll per probe (appended, never summarized —
    health is calculated from these atoms).
  - C6 — **the register link**: one Book. Scroll 1 =
    the drain record (§10.2): watermarks, register URL,
    last-drain cite. Secrets never live here (§10.4).

Queue records also touch three shipped homelands, by
reference rather than relocation: receipts and audits
live in ledgers 6.6.6 (§4.2, §7); bridge health is
dispatch-local (C5 above) because it is probed at
dispatch cadence, not telemetry cadence; persona
material stays in 4.4.4, cited by coordinate.

A node's queue is its own landscape (LAYER2 rule 7):
items an item's origin node owns live at the addresses
above in *its* state.phext. A working copy on an
executing node cites its origin in a `home:` field —
copies are read at their own addresses and never
re-address the origin's record.

### 3.2 The queue item record (one scroll, fields as lines)

```
queue item
title: <one line>
coordinate: 26.5.9/7.6.11/5.5.10
register: 112
origin: will | emise | cron | register | peer
enqueued: 2026-11-16T01:20:00-06:00
enqueued-by: aletheia-core A4E7277F…E755
state: queued
goal: <the daily-goal text this serves, or: standing>
value: 7
cost: 2
after: 11.11.11/2.1.4/1.1.1
attempts: 0
failures: 0
bundle-sha256: <sha256 of the canonical bundle, §3.4>
exit: <the exit condition, stated at enqueue>
```

Field rules: `coordinate:` is the Singular task
coordinate — the shared identity with tasks.phext (§12).
When an enqueuer cannot mint one (a phone quick
enqueue), the field is absent and the item's own address
is its identity until Emise reconciles it at review;
absence is printed as `coordinate: unminted`, never
silently blank. `register:` appears only on register-
born items and is never reused or renumbered (the
register's permanence rule). `value:`/`cost:` are the
ROI estimate (§5); absent = `roi: unestimated` in every
rendering. `attempts:` counts every attempt;
`failures:` counts *consecutive* failed attempts (§8).
Both are fields on the record, updated in the same save
as the transition they describe.

### 3.3 Chains and universes — attempts as sibling universes

The tree pins libphext 0.6.0 — the chains release (0x02
chain break, 0x03 chain close, `$N:` addressing) — and
no shipped module calls parse_chains/serialize_chains
yet (verified by grep over src/ @ 609bab65). The
dispatch core is the first chain-native consumer:

- **state.phext becomes a chain file.** Universe 1 is
  exactly today's state: every shipped verb reads
  universe 1 and sees the file it already knows (a
  single-phext read treats 0x02/0x03 as content — the
  v1.3.0 conformance finding — so universe-1 semantics
  are the compatibility floor, proven against foreign
  fixtures in slice 1's gates, §13).
- **Attempt N of an item lives in universe N+1** of the
  executing node's state chain, at the *same addresses*
  as the item record: `$2:11.11.11/2.1.4/1.1.1` is
  attempt 1's working copy of the item at
  11.11.11/2.1.4/1.1.1 — its assignment fields, its
  heartbeat lines, its checkpoint scrolls beside the
  record (Scroll 4+ of the same Book: one scroll per
  checkpoint, appended). Attempts are never merged into
  universe 1; the item record in universe 1 cites the
  highest attempt universe and its verdict. Diverged
  attempts are kept, both, addressable — Will's
  keep-both ruling made literal, the same ruling
  `evolve` already applies to persona forks at sibling
  Books.
- **Chains are the format's time**: the chain of a
  state file is its ordered editions. A pre-dispatch
  snapshot is the chain as it stood; recovery (§4.4)
  reads backward through universes, never through a
  sidecar. (No sidecars: LAYER2/house rule.)

### 3.4 The dispatch bundle — a chain on the wire

A dispatch is one chain, POSTed to the executing node's
`serve` at `/v1/dispatch`:

- **$1: — the spec universe.** At the bundle's own
  11.11.11/11.11.11/11.11.11: the bundle manifest
  scroll — item coordinate (or unminted), origin node
  name + fingerprint, per-universe SHA-256 list,
  required capability lines, value/cost/goal fields,
  `resume-from:` when this dispatch resumes (§8).
  Beside it at Scroll 2: the detached signature over
  the manifest's exact bytes (the releases cluster's
  manifest+signature pattern, shipped v1.1.0). The
  item record copy and the brief scroll ride at their
  home addresses in $1:.
- **$2:…$k: — context universes.** Input material as
  scroll selections *with provenance* — source path,
  source SHA-256, coordinate range — the phext-native-
  dispatch practice (the dispatch file's provenance
  header becomes a scroll in the universe it vouches
  for). A context universe is read-only evidence; an
  executor never writes into it.
- **Acceptance rule** (assess_offer's shape, same
  order): claimed signer is a registered peer →
  signature verifies under that peer's key → per-
  universe hashes match the manifest → platform/
  capability fit → dedup (§4.3). Any failure = a
  printed refusal and a refusal receipt on the
  *receiving* node; nothing is filed. Integrity and
  authenticity ride entirely on this signature path,
  which works today (§11).

## 4. Queue semantics — "never lost", made provable

### 4.1 Write-before-ack

`enqueue` returns — and prints the item address — only
after the state save that contains the item completes.
If the save fails, enqueue exits non-zero and prints no
address: an address Will or Emise holds is proof the
record is durable. The same rule binds `drain` (items
are filed before watermarks advance, §10.2) and every
transition verb.

### 4.2 Transitions and receipts are one write

Every change of an item's `state:` field lands in the
same state save as its receipt: a scroll in the
ledgers homeland, receipts cluster (6.6.6, Collection
2, shipped), citing the item address, the from→to
states, the actor, the evidence, and the bundle hash
where one exists. A transition without its receipt
cannot be represented — they are one write, so the
save either contains both or neither. Non-ping
closures additionally write an audit record (§7) in
the same save.

### 4.3 Idempotent dispatch, at-least-once delivery

Bundle identity = (item address, bundle-sha256). The
manifest's hash is over the canonical chain bytes, so
re-dispatching an unchanged item reproduces the same
identity. A receiving node that already holds an
assignment for that identity answers with its existing
acceptance receipt — delivery is at-least-once,
execution is once per identity. A *changed* brief is
a new bundle hash and a new attempt (a new universe),
never a mutation of an accepted attempt.

### 4.4 Terminal states, evidence, and recovery

- Terminal states (`done`, `cancelled`) are reachable
  only through a transition whose receipt carries
  **live-surface evidence**: what was checked (repo,
  PR, file, service, node), what it showed, the
  verdict — the F4 evidence shape (§7), whether or
  not a human ever looks.
- No verb deletes a queue record, an attempt universe,
  a receipt, or an audit. Retirement is a status line
  and an index note (LAYER2 rule 5).
- **Crash mid-save:** state saves are whole-file
  atomic replacements (the shipped lattice behavior
  the update path already relies on); a torn write is
  impossible by construction, and doctor's integrity
  pass (§4.5) is the check that would catch a
  violation if one ever appeared.
- **Wipe / rebirth:** a node's state chain is
  archivable as a chain (the pre-v1.3.0 precedent:
  old states preserved as chain-readable archives).
  On rebirth, the node re-registers its peers, then
  *re-pulls* its open items: register-born items are
  re-derived from the register (§10 — the register
  never lost them), coordinator items are re-sent by
  their origin from its own state, and every re-pull
  is a receipt. An item whose origin is also gone is
  reconstructed from its tasks.phext coordinate +
  register number and re-enqueued marked
  `origin: reconstructed` — visible, never silent.

### 4.5 The integrity reconciliation (doctor)

`qexo doctor` gains a queue section, calculated from
the records on every run (never stored): items by
state; terminal items lacking a receipt (must be 0);
receipts citing no live item (must be 0); assignments
whose heartbeat is stale (each must be on the F5 path,
§8); audit records for every non-ping closure in the
window (a closure without one is a finding, §7).
Any nonzero must-be-0 count is a doctor failure of
the same class as a missing homeland index.

### 4.6 The queue state machine

States: `queued`, `dispatched`, `running`, `waiting`,
`paused`, `paused-auto`, `done`, `cancelled`.
(`dispatched` = bundle accepted, execution not yet
evidenced; `waiting` = blocked on Will, cites its
register number, dispatch skips it; `paused` = manual.)

Legal transitions (actor → transition):

- enqueue → `queued` (any authorized enqueuer)
- `queued` → `dispatched` (dispatch verb; acceptance
  receipt)
- `dispatched` → `running` (executor's first heartbeat)
- `running` → `running` (checkpoint appended)
- `running` → `done` (executor's completion receipt +
  coordinator's live-surface verification, §7)
- `running`/`dispatched` → `queued` (failure: attempt
  closes failed, failures+1, re-dispatch per §8)
- `queued` → `waiting` (a Will-gate is identified;
  register number cited)
- `waiting` → `queued` (Will acts; the register
  resolution or his word is the evidence)
- any non-terminal → `paused` (manual pause, note)
- `paused` → `queued` (resume)
- failure #3 (consecutive) → `paused-auto` (§8)
- `paused-auto` → `queued` (manual resume only)
- any non-terminal → `cancelled` (Will/Emise, note
  required, register write-back when register-born)

Illegal combinations the design rules out — each is a
doctor finding if it ever appears in a state file:

1. A terminal item with no receipt, or a receipt
   whose evidence fields are empty.
2. An item `running` under two live assignments —
   **single-assignment invariant**: exactly one
   attempt universe is authoritative, the highest;
   a stale executor's late receipt is filed as
   evidence and cannot move state (fencing by
   attempt number).
3. `paused-auto` with `failures:` < 3, or `queued`
   with `failures:` ≥ 3 and no intervening verified
   progress or manual resume (§8's reset rules).
4. `cancelled` with no note, or cancelled by an
   executor (actors are checked, not trusted).
5. `waiting` with no register number cited.
6. An item whose `bundle-sha256:` does not match the
   canonical bundle its record describes.
7. A queue item in universe 1 whose cited attempt
   universe does not exist.

## 5. Prioritization — ROI ordering and the latency budget

### 5.1 The ordering rule (calculated, never stored)

The queue's order is computed at every read from the
item records, in this exact sequence:

1. **Daily-goal impact first.** Items whose `goal:`
   field names Will's current daily goal sort ahead of
   `standing` items, which sort ahead of background
   items. The daily goal is a field on the node's
   config-side records, set by Emise at the morning
   synthesis; an item's class is a field on the item.
2. **ROI ratio next.** `value ÷ cost` from the item's
   recorded fields, descending. The ratio is always
   calculated from the two stored atoms, never stored
   itself (the house rule: rollups are calculated,
   never hand-written). Items with no estimate sort
   below estimated items *within the same goal class*,
   ordered among themselves by age, and render as
   `roi: unestimated` — the gap is visible, not hidden.
3. **Age last.** `enqueued:` ascending — oldest first.
   Recency is never priority (the register's
   anti-recency rule, applied to the queue).

### 5.2 Who may re-order, and how it is audited

Will may re-order from any surface (CLI, phone, the
register). Emise may re-order as coordinator. Nobody
else: executors never touch ordering fields. A
re-order is an edit to `goal:`/`value:`/`cost:`/`pin:`
fields **plus** an audit record (§7) naming the actor,
the old keys, the new keys, and the reason — in the
same save (§4.2's one-write rule). A `pin:` field
(`pin: head` / `pin: after <address>`) is the explicit
override; pins render as pins in `qexo queue`, never
as if the calculation produced them.

### 5.3 The latency budget (P1, made measurable)

- enqueue → ack: the local save. Budget ≤ 1 s.
- enqueue → first action: the acceptance receipt of
  the first dispatch. Budget ≤ 10 s on a healthy
  tailnet for an item dispatched at enqueue time.
- What keeps it true: no polling interval stands in
  the path (dispatch is a push, §3.4); placement
  inputs are already in state (peers, telemetry,
  bridge health); the bundle is built from scrolls
  already local. Where a budget is missed, the two
  timestamps are on the records and the miss is a
  bench finding (§14), not an anecdote.

### 5.4 Phone authority

The Android node enqueues, dispatches, pauses,
resumes, and cancels with exactly the authority of
any node — it holds a node key, its signature is the
credential, and §2.3's quick actions are the same
verbs. There is no "mobile-lite" queue: the phone's
queue view is the calculated order over the same
records, fetched from the coordinator node or held
in its own state, read at their own addresses either
way (LAYER2 rule 7).

## 6. Placement and execution

- **Placement inputs**, all already in state: peer
  records (3.3.3 homeland — endpoint, host, status),
  telemetry (8.8.8 — disk observations today;
  temperature joins it as a probe field in slice 4),
  and the item's `capability:` requirements (from the
  brief: e.g. `capability: cargo`, `capability:
  gpu-free`, `capability: bridge:claude`).
- **Target selection — the fair draw (Will,
  2026-11-16 20:30 CST, verbatim):** "you need a
  round-robin scheduler - don't always focus on
  using aletheia-core" / "use qexo to dispatch
  fairly - random selection per round". The node
  that runs an item is chosen by a **random draw
  per round** from the eligible set. Eligibility —
  all four required:
  (a) the node is **reachable**;
  (b) its **load/temperature gates pass** (the
  ≤75 °C gate below; load measured from telemetry,
  never assumed);
  (c) **no resident agent is displaced** — a node
  whose resident agent is at work is ineligible
  for work that would displace it;
  (d) **data locality** — the item's inputs are on
  the node, or cheap to stage; the staging cost is
  recorded on the placement receipt, never
  guessed.
  An item's `capability:` requirements filter the
  set the same way, before the draw.
  **aletheia-core is one peer among equals in the
  draw.** Its ratified roles (drainer/coordinator
  host, §15 Q1; fleet signer) give it **no**
  execution preference.
- **Composition:** §5's ordering and the heavy
  FIFO below decide **which job goes next**; the
  draw decides **which node runs it**; the N=4
  heavy cap still binds. The draw never reorders
  the queue, and the queue never names a node.
- **The draw is receipted:** the placement receipt
  (the assignment record, C3) records the eligible
  set as drawn from, every excluded node with its
  exclusion reason (which of (a)–(d), or
  capability), and the node drawn. Fairness is
  auditable after the fact; §7's audit surface
  reads these receipts.
- **Empty eligible set:** a round with no eligible
  node is a named outcome — the item stays queued,
  the round is receipted, placement retries next
  round. There is **no** silent fall back to
  aletheia-core, or to any default node, ever.
- **Thermal gate:** a node at or above **75 °C**
  fails eligibility (b) and is excluded from the
  round's draw, its exclusion named in the
  placement receipt — never a silent skip.
- **Small units:** a brief that names more than one
  unit of work is enqueued as one item per unit with
  `after:` chaining — the queue, not the brief, is
  the unit scheduler (the house small-units rule for
  ranch hardware).
- **Heavy-work FIFO:** at most **4 heavy items**
  running fleet-wide (the house N=4 rule), first-in
  first-out among heavy items; light items are not
  blocked by the heavy queue. Heavy is a brief field
  (`heavy: yes`), defaulting to no.
- **Heartbeat:** the executor updates its assignment
  record (C3) and its attempt universe's record
  scroll with a `heartbeat:` line every 2 minutes of
  execution. The origin reads them over the shipped
  scroll route (`/v1/scroll/<coord>`) — no new
  transport.
- **Checkpointing:** a checkpoint is an appended
  scroll in the attempt's Book (Scroll 4+, §3.3):
  what is done, what remains, where the outputs live
  (paths/coordinates/hashes). Checkpoints are the
  resume currency (§8) and the progress evidence
  (§7).
- **Teleport:** on node loss, the origin re-dispatches
  the same bundle identity (§4.3) to a node drawn in
  a fresh fair-draw round (eligibility + draw, above)
  with `resume-from: $<n>:<checkpoint-address>`
  in the manifest. Teleport changes the attempt,
  never the fairness rule. The new attempt is the next
  universe. The old node's assignment is fenced by
  attempt number (§4.6, illegal combination 2).

## 7. F4 — auto-verification and the audit view

Will's amendment, verbatim in substance: every
verification that ends in a **non-ping** — an item
closed or suppressed because its live surface showed
it settled — writes an audit record, and Will can
view those audits. Silence without evidence is not
permitted.

### 7.1 The audit record (one scroll, fields as lines)

Audits live in the ledgers homeland as a new cluster:
**6.6.6, Collection 9 — audits** (Collections 2–8 are
the shipped ledger kinds; 9 is next, assigned here).
One Book per audit, in sequence:

```
audit
kind: non-ping-close | reorder | auto-pause |
      register-close | verification
item: 11.11.11/2.1.4/1.1.1
checked: <the live surface — repo/PR/file/service/
         node — named exactly>
showed: <what the surface showed, quoted or hashed>
verdict: closed-settled | suppressed | confirmed-open
actor: <node + fingerprint, or emise>
at: 2026-11-16T01:31:00-06:00
```

### 7.2 The rules

- A `done` transition whose evidence came from a
  live-surface check (§4.4) writes its audit in the
  same save. A closure with no audit is illegal
  combination 1's twin — a doctor finding (§4.5).
- The register drain (§10) is an audit consumer from
  its first day: a browser-side Done/Cancel that
  closes a queue item is a `register-close` audit,
  citing the resolution's seq as evidence.
- Re-orders (§5.2) and auto-pauses (§8) are audits,
  not just receipts: receipts say *what happened*;
  audits say *what was checked and why it was
  allowed to happen quietly*.
- `qexo audit` (§2.1) is Will's window: list with
  filters, show one in full, at phone width under
  `--app`. If it isn't in the audit cluster, it
  didn't happen quietly — it didn't happen.

## 8. F5 — self-resuming tasks, capped at 3

Will's amendment: a task auto-resumes at most 3
times; on the 3rd failure it auto-pauses and
surfaces to Will.

- **Failure detection**, two evidences, either
  sufficient: (a) heartbeat miss — 3 consecutive
  missed beats (6 minutes) with the node's serve
  also unreachable or silent; (b) exit evidence —
  the executor's own failed receipt for the attempt.
  Detection is a coordinator act (the origin node,
  or Emise's drain pass for coordinator items).
- **Resume:** re-dispatch per §6 (teleport) with
  `resume-from:` citing the last checkpoint. Resume
  is within the item's original scope — the bundle
  is the same identity (§4.3); a resume never
  widens a brief.
- **The counter:** `failures:` on the item record
  counts *consecutive* failed attempts. It resets
  to 0 on exactly two events: a **verified progress
  receipt** (a checkpoint that advances the work,
  verified by the coordinator's read of the cited
  output — not the executor's say-so), or a
  **manual resume** (§2.1), which writes its audit.
  `attempts:` never resets; both numbers are always
  on the record, so the history is calculated, not
  summarized.
- **Auto-pause:** the transition that would make
  `failures:` 3 instead lands the item in
  `paused-auto`, writes the audit (kind:
  auto-pause, with all three failures' evidence
  cited), and pins the item to the head of every
  queue rendering (§2.1).
- **Surfacing:** the auto-pause reaches Will by
  three paths, all from records: the pinned queue
  item (CLI + phone), the audit view, and Emise's
  progress tick, which reads the queue during its
  drain and carries a `paused-auto` item into the
  Waiting-on-Will register through her existing
  register process — the tick is the bridge between
  qexo's surfacing and Will's, and it cites the
  item address when it does.

## 9. F6 — stable bridges to all inference sources

Will's F6: qexo maintains durable, health-checked
bridges to every inference source it can route to;
dispatch routes only over bridges verified live;
failover is recorded as receipts.

### 9.1 The bridge record (11.11.11, Collection 5)

```
bridge
name: claude
kind: claude | grok | chatgpt | ollama | tlm |
      slm | llm | sq | custom
endpoint: <where the source is reached — URL,
          mailbox port, gateway path>
capability: <one line per capability, repeatable>
burn: metered | never
reset: <for metered cloud substrates: the reset
        cliff in text, e.g. "Wed 22:00 America/
        Chicago" — the substrate-steering record>
freshness: 900
status: active | retired
```

Health is **calculated** from the Book's health-log
scrolls (one per probe: at, probe kind, result,
latency) — never a stored `health: ok` field. A
bridge is *live* iff its latest probe succeeded
within its `freshness:` window (seconds; 900
default).

### 9.2 Routing and failover

- An item that needs inference names its acceptable
  bridges in preference order (`bridge:` lines in
  the brief). Dispatch probes the first choice at
  dispatch time if its evidence is stale — routing
  only over a bridge verified live *for this
  dispatch*, and the probe + choice are cited in
  the dispatch receipt.
- Failover: if the chosen bridge fails at dispatch
  or mid-execution, the next preference is probed
  and used; every failover is a receipt (from, to,
  evidence). A dispatch with no live bridge waits
  `queued` with the reason on the record — it does
  not fall back to an unlisted source.
- **ChatGPT is a reservoir, never a burn target**
  (substrate-steering rule): its bridge record
  carries `burn: never`; dispatch never routes work
  to it. Context deposits (planning material) are
  not dispatches.
- **Local inference only where Will configures it**
  (standing rule, 2026-11-12): qexo never creates
  an ollama/local bridge on its own. A local bridge
  exists only when Will (or Emise at his direction)
  runs `bridge --add`. The shipped `host`/`message`
  Ollama edge remains as-is underneath.
- **The model line is first-class.** Per Will's
  stated premise (2026-11-14, recorded as his
  premise, not a verified result): "assume our TLM,
  SLM, and LLM projects will outperform all prior
  attempts, since we can both read and write the
  data." Bridge kinds `tlm` (Iam), `slm`
  (Liora'veth), `llm` (Zelari) are in the kind enum
  from the first bridge slice, so the local line
  registers as bridges the day each can answer a
  probe — no schema change waits for them.

## 10. Register integration — qexo is the drainer

Binding direction (Will, 2026-11-16 01:08 CST):
"have qexo integrate with register.phext.io and
then ship it." The register is the durable,
never-lost queue made visible; qexo consumes it,
executes against it, and writes outcomes back.
Grounding: wbic16/register @ 0927b85, register-
service/README.md + regsvc/app.py + regsvc/store.py,
read 2026-11-16 (provenance, §16).

### 10.1 What the service actually is (as read)

- Two append-only JSONL atom logs, each atom
  stamped with a monotonic `seq` (its line number,
  from 1) and `ts`. Atoms are never edited or
  deleted; rollups are computed from atoms.
- `POST /api/entries` (browser session + CSRF)
  appends `{type: entry, text, user, ts, seq}` —
  free-text quick entries. Text is required and
  length-capped server-side.
- `GET /api/entries/pending?after_seq=N` and
  `GET /api/resolutions?after_seq=N` return
  `{atoms, last_seq}` for atoms with seq > N —
  session auth, **or** the drain token (§10.4).
- `POST /api/items/{n}/done` and
  `POST /api/items/{n}/cancel` (session + CSRF
  only, today) validate `n` against
  register-numbers.json and append
  `{type: resolution, register_number, user,
  outcome: done|cancelled, note?, ts, seq}`.
  Duplicate resolutions for one number are kept in
  the log and treated as no-ops at drain time.
- The service **never writes the register of
  record** (waiting-on-will.md). Applying atoms to
  the register is the drainer's job — today the
  progress tick's, blocked on the drain token;
  under this spec, qexo's.

### 10.2 Per-user scope (binding, 2026-11-16 01:12 CST)

Will's direction, verbatim: "just move the data
for register.phext.io so it is scoped by the user
login." The integration is specced against the
**per-user** semantics, not the current global
pile:

- Register data lives in per-login scopes (LDAP
  uid). Existing data migrates to Will's scope.
- The register qexo reads, drains, and writes
  back is **the authenticated user's own scope**.
  Will's nodes act on Will's scope; the drain
  returns only that scope's entries and
  resolutions.
- Every credential in §10.4 inherits the scope:
  the qexo service token acts on Will's scope
  only. A token presented against another scope
  authorizes nothing.
- **Named dependency:** the per-user scoping is a
  register-service change being built and staged
  separately (one staged update on awsbox that
  Will runs). The integration slice (§13, slice
  3) depends on it explicitly: qexo's drain is
  written and gated against the scoped API, and
  the slice does not close until the staged
  service update is live. The spec assumes the
  scoped semantics and does not design a fallback
  to the global pile — if the service change
  slips, the slice waits, visibly, as a
  dependency state on this task's record.

### 10.3 The drain (qexo's job)

`qexo drain` runs on the designated drainer node —
**aletheia-core** (the always-on hub; the phone and
laptop never hold register credentials). Cadence:
the progress tick fires it (§13, cutover C3); it
is also runnable by hand from any surface.

One drain pass:

1. Read the drain record (11.11.11, C6): entries
   watermark W_e, resolutions watermark W_r.
2. `GET /api/entries/pending?after_seq=W_e` with
   the drain credential → entry atoms of Will's
   scope. Each atom becomes a queue item:
   `origin: register`, `register-seq: <seq>`,
   brief = the entry text, `enqueued:` from the
   atom's ts, `enqueued-by:` the atom's user.
   Emise's triage (her existing tick judgment)
   enriches coordinate/value/cost/goal fields
   afterward — enrichment is a field edit + audit,
   never a rewrite of the entry text.
3. `GET /api/resolutions?after_seq=W_r` → 
   resolution atoms. A resolution for register
   number n closes the matching queue item
   (`done`, or `cancelled` with the atom's note)
   with a `register-close` audit citing the
   resolution seq as its live-surface evidence
   (§7.2). A resolution citing a number with no
   queue item is filed as an audit with no home —
   visible, never silently dropped. Duplicates are
   no-ops, per the service's own contract.
4. Only after every item and audit of the pass is
   saved (§4.1) do the watermarks advance, in the
   same save. A crashed drain re-reads the same
   atoms and dedups by `register-seq:` / resolution
   seq — draining is idempotent by construction.

Numbered register items themselves keep their
authority where it already lives: register numbers
are minted by the register of record
(waiting-on-will.md and its page generator,
register-numbers.json) — qexo never invents a
number. When Emise registers a wait, the queue
item is enqueued with `register: <n>`; when qexo
closes such an item, §10.4's write-back records
the resolution against that number.

### 10.4 Credential leg (named, exact)

Auth as it stands (from the service's README and
code): browser writes use an **LDAP session +
CSRF token**. Headless reads use a dedicated
**drain token** — env `REGSVC_DRAIN_TOKEN`,
presented as header `X-Drain-Token`, authorizing
**only** the two GET drains, never writes, never
login. The token's value lives server-side in
`/etc/register-service/env` on awsbox, generated
by install-on-box.sh. **Emise does not hold it** —
only Will's sudo reads that file — and the tick
drain has been blocked on exactly this.

What qexo needs, and the smallest shape that
provides it:

- **Reads:** the existing drain token, unchanged.
  One provisioned copy for the drainer node.
- **Write-back:** resolutions (done/cancel with
  Will's note) are writes, and the drain token
  cannot write. The smallest addition to the
  register service: **one scoped service token
  for qexo** — a second env-configured token
  (working name `REGSVC_QEXO_TOKEN`, header
  `X-Qexo-Token`, constant-time compare,
  fail-closed when unset — the drain token's own
  pattern in app.py) authorizing
  `POST /api/items/{n}/done` and
  `POST /api/items/{n}/cancel`, recording `user`
  as the token's identity (e.g. `qexo`) on the
  resolution atom. Per §10.2, the token is bound
  to **Will's scope**: it closes items in Will's
  scope and no other. No login, no session
  powers. This is a register-service sub-slice
  with its own PR and tests in wbic16/register,
  inside integration slice 3.
- **Entries-append (ratified scope, Q2).** As
  drafted, the token above was resolutions-only;
  at ratification Will widened it (§15, Q2,
  2026-11-16): the token also authorizes
  `POST /api/entries` on Will's scope, so items
  born on the phone appear in the register web
  view. Entries appended under the token record
  `user` as the token's identity, same as
  resolutions. The scope stops there: no other
  scope, no login, no session powers — and the
  scope is not widenable by configuration
  (§10.5).
- **One Will pass provisions everything.** The
  staged on-box update Will runs for the per-user
  scoping (§10.2) is the same pass that (a)
  generates/rotates the qexo service token,
  (b) surfaces the drain token value to Will,
  and (c) places both values on the drainer node
  by Will's hand (ssh/scp from the box). All
  credential provisioning lands in that single
  pass — there is no second credential errand.
- **Hard constraints.** No flow may require a
  token value to pass through chat, and none may
  pass through a phext — so the credentials do
  **not** live in state.phext's config record
  (state scrolls are served to peers; a secret in
  state is a published secret). On the drainer
  node they live in an **env file beside the
  state**: `/var/lib/qexo/qexo.env`, mode 0600,
  root-owned, read by the binary at runtime
  (`QEXO_ENV` overrides the path for dev/tests).
  Receipts and audits cite credentials by name
  only (`credential: drain-token (env)`), never
  by value. Rotation = Will re-runs the
  provisioning pass; qexo holds no rotation
  machinery.

### 10.5 What the integration deliberately does not do

qexo posts register entries only under the
ratified token scope (§10.4, Q2): items born on
the phone are appended to Will's scope so they
appear in the register web view; every other
entry stays browser-born or Emise-born. (The
draft's stronger line — "never posts quick
entries; write-back is resolutions only" — was
superseded by Will's Q2 decision at
ratification, 2026-11-16.) qexo never writes
waiting-on-will.md (Emise's register process
keeps that pen, fed by drain results); never
mints register numbers; never widens the
service token's scope beyond the ratified scope
by configuration. Each "never" is a test in
slice 3's gates (§13).

## 11. DEPENDENCY FLAG — encrypt_to/decrypt is broken

**Named dependency, not a footnote.** Task
26.5.9/6.5.9/5.5.8 (filed by the v1.6.1
verification pass, ~/workspace/qexo-task/
testing-v161/REPORT.md, leg 6): `crypto.rs`'s
`encrypt_to`/`decrypt` — the signed-then-sealed
persona keychain exchange the code's own doc
comment names as DESIGN §4.7 — **cannot complete
an exchange with any key qexo generates, imports,
or migrates**. Proven three ways in the pass:
encrypting to a qexo-born key fails ("EdDSALegacy
is only used for signing" — qexo's keys are
sign-only); encrypting to an imported encryption-
capable correspondent fails identically (the rpgp
call tests the primary key and never selects the
encryption subkey); decrypting through a migrated
subkey-carrying keyring fails ("missing key" —
the migration path yields no usable encryption
secret). Sign/verify are unaffected and proven
(fleet rollouts v1.5.0–v1.6.1 ride them).

**What depends on the fix, and waits for it:**

- **Sealed context universes.** A bundle context
  universe (§3.4) sealed to the executing node's
  key — the form a bundle must take when its
  context carries persona keychain material or
  anything that must not be readable by every
  peer that can fetch scrolls. Until the fix:
  bundles travel signed and hash-verified but
  **unsealed**, and briefs must not carry material
  that requires sealing. This is stated on the
  bundle manifest (`sealed: no`) so the property
  is never implied.
- **Persona-bearing dispatch.** Dispatching work
  that requires moving a persona's keychain to
  the executing node (the §4.7 exchange's whole
  purpose) cannot be specced past this point
  until the fix lands. Persona *references*
  (coordinate cites, boot from material already
  resident on the node) are unaffected.
- **Sealed checkpoints.** Checkpoint scrolls
  containing keychain material inherit the same
  wait.

**What does NOT depend on it, and ships without
it:** everything else in this spec. Queue records,
bundles' integrity and authenticity (signatures —
proven in production), dedup, placement, resume,
audits, bridges, the register integration, and
the drain credentials (env-file transport by
Will's hand, §10.4 — deliberately not a phext
flow). The fix is its own slice under its own
task; when it lands, sealed universes are a
follow-on slice that flips `sealed:` to a real
option. Nothing in slices 1–9 (§13) waits for it,
and no slice designs around it silently — any
brief that would need sealing is refused at
enqueue with the dependency named.

## 12. Boundaries — tasks.phext, and the deploy seam

**tasks.phext** (the task register) and the qexo
queue are different records of different things,
sharing one identity: the Singular task
coordinate.

- tasks.phext is the coordinator's durable record
  of *what was decided and what came of it* —
  every task Will issues, its notes and results,
  kept on the Muse VM under the task-register
  process. It is Will-facing history.
- The qexo queue is the nodes' record of *what is
  executing* — items, attempts, heartbeats,
  receipts. It is machine-facing state.
- Flow: a decided task is enqueued citing its
  coordinate; queue receipts flow back and the
  coordinator writes the outcome into the task's
  scroll (the existing register process, step 6:
  the coordinator reconciles delegate reports
  into the record). Neither register replaces the
  other; a task may live in tasks.phext with no
  queue item (planning-stage, chat-layer work),
  and that is a normal state, not drift.

**Deploy seam** (restating §0): release manifests
and dispatch bundles are different trust objects.
The DevSecOps generalization (extended `artifact:`
stanzas, serve-route naming) proceeds under its
own ratification; if it lands first, dispatch
reuses its serve-route generalization rather than
growing a second one — named here so the two
specs cannot silently diverge.

## 13. Cutover and build slices

### 13.1 The cutover principle

**Planning stays in the chats.** Muse.ai channels
remain the planning/discussion layer; qexo becomes
the dispatch/execution layer; the register is the
shared durable queue between them (§0). The chats
themselves do not move. What cuts over is
execution plumbing — three surfaces, each phased,
each phase independently revertible: a phase is
additive (the old surface keeps working until the
phase's gate passes), and reverting is "stop
enqueueing" — the old surface was never made
unreadable.

### 13.2 The three surfaces

**(a) Subagent briefs → dispatch bundles.**
Today: the main thread writes a self-contained
brief; a child executes; a handoff returns; the
coordinator verifies and reconciles (the
dispatch-to-subagents skill). Cutover: the brief
becomes a bundle (§3.4) — the brief text is the
spec universe; phext material the worker must
read rides as context universes in the
phext-native-dispatch form (selection + provenance
header as a scroll, the existing practice made
structural). The item is enqueued before the
child is spawned, so the dispatch itself is a
queue record with a receipt. Results return as
the attempt's result scrolls + completion
receipt; the coordinator's role is unchanged —
coordinate, decide, verify decisive results,
reconcile into tasks.phext. Honest seam: Muse
subagents are not qexo nodes — in this phase
qexo *tracks and receipts* the dispatch while
execution still runs on the harness; ranch/Choir
execution (§6) is where qexo also *carries* it.
The tracking phase alone buys P3 for briefs:
a brief can no longer exist only in a chat.

**(b) /chat mailboxes → the mailbox homeland.**
Today: one Markdown file per message in per-seat
inbox/outbox on aletheia-core, archived into
mailbox.phext by the tidy tool; dispatch = a
mailbox message; uptake verified from outboxes
(the choir-mailbox-dispatch skill). Cutover:
the *ledger* migrates, the doorbell stays. A
Choir dispatch becomes a queue item whose bundle
hash + item address ride in the mailbox message
headers (the from:/re: convention gains
`qexo: <address>` and `bundle: <sha256>` lines);
uptake is still read from the seat's outbox, but
the item's state machine (§4.6) is what tracks
it, and the completion receipt closes it.
mailbox.phext archives continue untouched — the
seats cannot read state.phext, so the mailbox
files remain their surface; what moves is
Emise's dispatch bookkeeping, out of message
archaeology and into queue records. Per-seat
mailboxes on other ranch nodes follow the same
header convention when their seats dispatch.

**(c) Cron agents → scheduled dispatch entries.**
Today: crons fire agents that carry their own
bookkeeping (e.g. the progress tick's state
JSON). Cutover: a cron fires `qexo enqueue`
(from a schedule record, 11.11.11 C4) or `qexo
drain`; qexo owns the execution state from
there — attempts, failures, auto-pause — and
the cron's private state files retire into
queue records. First consumer: the progress
tick itself, whose drain (§10.3) becomes
`qexo drain` and whose per-request bookkeeping
becomes queue + audit reads. A cron that finds
qexo unavailable falls back to its current
behavior and files the failure as its own
finding — the fallback is a phase, not a
permanent dual system; the phase gate is 14
consecutive days of clean qexo-owned runs.

### 13.3 Build slices — in order, one at a time

Each slice: gates on aletheia-core, house style
(foreign-authored fixtures, byte-inert `--check`
proofs, negative legs), its own PR, Will merges.
Register integration lands **before the ship**
(Will: "integrate … and then ship it").

1. **Chain state foundation.** state.phext as a
   chain file (universe 1 = today's state,
   byte-compatible reads); `$N:` addressing in
   select/get/show; homeland 11.11.11 laid down
   (index + top-up by doctor, the v1.4.0 lessons
   pattern); item records + `enqueue`/`queue`/
   `show` (local only). Gates: conformance census
   identical on the three foreign files (editor-
   append fixture, CYOA, SQ-written); a v1.7.1-
   born state fixture opens and operates
   unchanged; `--check` paths byte-inert.
2. **Queue semantics.** The state machine (§4.6)
   as code + doctor's integrity reconciliation
   (§4.5); receipts/audits in the same save;
   pause/resume/cancel; ROI ordering + `--explain`
   (§5). Gates: the illegal-combination fixtures
   (hand-corrupted states) each produce their
   doctor finding; transition/receipt atomicity
   proven by crash-mid-save fixtures.
3. **Register integration.** `qexo drain` +
   write-back (§10.3, §10.4) against the per-user
   scoped API (§10.2). Sub-slice in wbic16/
   register: the scoped qexo service token, own
   PR + tests. Dependencies, explicit: the
   staged per-user service update live on awsbox
   (Will's on-box leg) and the single credential
   provisioning pass (§10.4). Gates: drain
   idempotency (double-drain files nothing
   twice); watermark crash-safety; the token's
   legs, at the ratified Q2 scope (resolutions +
   entries-append, Will's scope only): positive
   leg — a token-appended entry appears in the
   register web view; negative legs (token
   cannot append entries to another scope,
   cannot read another scope, cannot write with
   the drain token, unset token fails closed);
   a foreign-scope fixture drains to empty.
4. **Wire dispatch.** Bundle format + `POST
   /v1/dispatch` + acceptance rule + dedup (§§3.4,
   4.3); placement (the fair draw over the
   eligible set, capability/thermal/FIFO, §6);
   heartbeat + checkpoints. Gates: two-node
   sandbox (accept, refuse-forged, refuse-stale,
   dedup-replay, thermal-refusal-with-receipt);
   a bundle authored by the Android build
   accepted by a Linux node (foreign-authored
   fixture, the conformance rule); **fairness
   legs (§6, Will 2026-11-16):** (i) repeated
   placements in the sandbox distribute across
   all eligible nodes — no node starved, none
   taking all rounds, within the tolerance the
   gate script states; (ii) exclusion legs — a
   node failing each eligibility criterion in
   turn (unreachable; at/above 75 °C; resident
   agent at work; inputs not stageable) is never
   drawn, and its exclusion reason appears in
   the placement receipt; (iii) empty-eligible-
   set leg — with every node excluded, dispatch
   queues with its receipt and places nothing,
   least of all on the coordinator by default.
5. **F5 resume.** Failure detection, teleport,
   the counter and its resets, auto-pause +
   surfacing (§8). Gates: kill-the-executor
   fixture (resume from checkpoint, scope
   unchanged); three-failure fixture lands in
   paused-auto with its audit; stale-executor
   late receipt cannot move state.
6. **F6 bridges.** Registry, health log, routing
   + failover receipts (§9); bridge kinds for the
   model line present from day one. Gates:
   stale-health bridge is not routed to; failover
   receipt chain complete; `burn: never` bridge
   refuses a work dispatch at enqueue.
7. **App surface + twin sync.** `--app` drivers
   for every §2.1 verb; launcher v3 quick actions
   (§2.3); **the twin-sync leg (binding,
   2026-11-16):** quarterexo's integration build
   ports to wbic16/qexo as its own PR — carrying
   the integration *and* the v1.7.1 platform-
   stamp content the twin is missing (the twin
   still stamps 1.7.0 and its exo tree carries
   no 1.7.1 content), Android deltas preserved
   through the port. Rationale, stated plainly:
   Will's phone is his main dispatch surface, so
   a ship the phone can't run is not a ship.
   Gates: scripted `--app` sessions on both
   repos' builds with identical transcripts;
   Android + windows-gnu full LINKs; the twin's
   tree diff against quarterexo is exactly the
   sanctioned Android deltas.
8. **Ship.** Version bump on both repos, PRs,
   Will merges, publish + fleet rollout through
   the signed channel — the ship carries the
   register integration aboard (the held
   v1.7.1-line release, task 40.9.7/11.9.17/
   8.8.11, is re-scoped by the main chat to this
   build). Gates: the house release gates +
   a fleet node draining the real register
   (Will's scope) as the rollout's closing
   proof.
9. **Cutover phases** (§13.2), in order (a)
   briefs, (b) mailboxes, (c) crons — each its
   own gate as specced there. Sealed bundles
   (§11) slot in as their own slice whenever
   the encrypt fix (26.5.9/6.5.9/5.5.8) has
   landed; they are not numbered here because
   they are not this spec's to schedule.

## 14. Test plan — keyed to the mission metrics

For consumption by the bench task (53.14.8/
18.8.27/6.9.10): every metric is calculated from
record atoms (timestamps, receipts, health
scrolls), never from a stored summary.

- **Intent→first-action latency** (P1): per item,
  `enqueued:` → first acceptance receipt time;
  reported as median + p95 over the trailing 7
  days, against the §5.3 budgets.
- **Queue integrity** (P3): doctor's reconciliation
  (§4.5) run as a bench probe — lost count (must
  be 0), terminal-without-receipt (0), orphan
  receipts (0); plus a weekly foreign-fixture
  drill: a state written by the *other* repo's
  build (Android ↔ Linux) reconciles clean.
- **Resume under the cap** (F5): resumed items
  reaching `done` ÷ all resumed items; auto-pause
  rate; mean attempts-to-done. Fixtures from
  slice 5's gates become the standing pack.
- **Bridge uptime** (F6): per bridge, successful
  probes ÷ all probes per window, from the health
  log scrolls; failover count beside it.
- **Dispatch fairness** (§6): per-node draw
  distribution over the window, counted from
  placement receipts (never sampled); exclusion-
  reason counts by criterion — (a) unreachable,
  (b) load/temperature, (c) resident agent,
  (d) data locality, plus capability; empty-round
  count beside them. A distribution drifting onto
  one node is a bench finding, not an anecdote.
- **Drain health** (§10): register high-water seq
  vs watermark (lag), entries→items latency,
  write-back success rate. A growing lag is a
  bench finding before it is a surprise.
- **Time dilation + progress-per-token** are the
  bench task's own headline bars (task 24.5.8/
  12.6.6/7.4.8); dispatch feeds them its per-item
  timestamps and outcomes as atoms.

## 15. Open questions for Will — answered at ratification

Only genuine decisions, each with a
recommendation. **All six were answered by Will,
OBO, in the qexo chat on 2026-11-16
(02:21–02:23 CST)**; each question below carries
its outcome. Five were ratified as recommended;
Q2 was decided against the draft's
recommendation, and the spec was amended to the
ratified scope at close-out (§§10.4, 10.5, 13).

1. **Drainer node.** Spec designates
   aletheia-core. *Recommend: ratify* — it is the
   always-on hub and the fleet signer; the phone
   and laptop should never hold register
   credentials.
   **Outcome: RATIFIED as recommended** —
   aletheia-core is the drainer.
2. **Service-token scope.** Specced as
   resolutions-only (done/cancel, Will's scope).
   The alternative — also entries-append, so
   phone-born items appear in the register web
   view — widens the credential. *Recommend:
   resolutions-only now*; revisit if the web
   view's absence is felt.
   **Outcome: DECIDED AGAINST the draft's
   recommendation** — Will widened the token:
   resolutions (done/cancel) PLUS entries-append
   on Will's scope, so phone-born items appear
   in the register web view. §§10.4, 10.5, and
   the §13 slice-3 gates carry the ratified
   scope.
3. **Auto-pause surfacing.** Specced as queue pin
   + audit + the tick carrying it into the
   Waiting-on-Will register. Should an auto-pause
   also write anything to the register service
   directly? *Recommend: no* — resolutions close
   items; a pause is not a closure, and the tick
   already carries it.
   **Outcome: RATIFIED as recommended** — an
   auto-pause writes nothing to the register
   service directly; the tick carries it.
4. **ROI scale.** value/cost as integers 1–9,
   enqueuer-recorded. *Recommend: ratify* — the
   ratio only needs to order a queue, and small
   integers keep estimates honest and quick.
   **Outcome: RATIFIED as recommended** —
   value/cost are integers 1–9.
5. **Heartbeat numbers.** 2-minute beat, 3 missed
   = failure. *Recommend: start here*, tune from
   bench data (§14) rather than by argument.
   **Outcome: RATIFIED as recommended** — start
   at 2 min / 3 missed; tuning deferred to bench
   data.
6. **The 11.11.11 homeland.** Dispatch's anchor
   is proposed, per LAYER2's own rule that
   placements are ratified by use. *Recommend:
   ratify the anchor* so slice 1 can lay it down;
   the alternative is overloading plan (5.5.5),
   which already has a job.
   **Outcome: RATIFIED as recommended** —
   11.11.11 is dispatch & queue's homeland; plan
   (5.5.5) is not overloaded.

