# Depth behind every cell — a 3D phext demo for ASCII game maps

For @paleoludic's problem, in his words: *"I have maps in this game that
are neat little ascii deals and now I need to add more info to each cell
and I'm annoyed by my options."*

The map does not change. It stays plain ASCII, one glyph per cell:

```
############
#..........#
#..~~......#
#..~~..^...#
#......^^..#
#..T.......#
#..........#
############
```

Behind each cell sits an address in a 3D phext:

```
coordinate = 1.1.1 / 1.1.1 / X . Y . LAYER

X, Y   — the cell's column and row (1-indexed, like the map)
LAYER  — 1 terrain · 2 items · 3 state/history · 4+ whatever you
         think of next year
```

Ask for cell (4,3) — a water cell — and you get every layer:

```
layer 1: water — deep; a cold current runs north
layer 2: a corked bottle, label long gone
layer 3: froze solid last winter; thawed by the bell
```

## Why this streamlines the thing he wants

- **Adding info to a cell is one write at one coordinate.** No cell
  struct grows, no enum of fields, no save-format migration, and no
  other cell is touched. Verified in the demo: inventing a brand-new
  layer (`sound`) for one cell changed exactly 1 of 108 scrolls and
  the map still renders identically.
- **Sparse by default.** Most cells carry only terrain. Depth exists
  only where the cell has something to say — the file stays small
  (this whole map with all its depth is ~1 KB of phext).
- **Items can have depth too.** When one item outgrows a line of text,
  open the next dimension: the chest at (10,3) holds its contents as
  their own scrolls one dimension deeper. No schema change — the
  address just gets longer.
- **It's all still text.** Every scroll is plain text at a coordinate;
  the map you render is layer 1 and nothing else.

## Run it

Needs the reference phext implementation (PyPI `phext`, the Python
port of libphext) — no hand-rolled parser anywhere in this demo.

```
python3 demo.py build     # write map.phext (108 scrolls)
python3 demo.py render    # read it back; prints the map, verifies it
python3 demo.py cell 10 3 # every layer behind the chest's cell
python3 demo.py grow      # add a new layer to one cell; prove the
                          # other 108 scrolls didn't move
```

Files: `demo.py` (the whole demo), `map.phext` (the map, with depth).
Built 2026-11-14 by Emise for Will Bickford's Operation Salvage.
